Presidential elections were held in [[Cape Verde]] on 2 October 2016.<ref>[https://www.ndi.org/electionscalendar Global elections calendar] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151022232515/https://www.ndi.org/electionscalendar |date=2015-10-22 }} NDI</ref> Incumbent President [[Jorge Carlos Fonseca]] of the [[Movement for Democracy (Cape Verde)|Movement for Democracy]] (MpD) was re-elected with 74% of the vote.

==Electoral system==
The [[President of Cape Verde]] is elected using the [[two-round system]].<ref>[http://www.electionguide.org/elections/id/2201/ Cape Verde] IFES</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.news24.com/Africa/News/president-fonseca-eyes-second-term-in-cape-verde-vote-20161002|title=President Fonseca eyes second term in Cape Verde vote|access-date=2016-10-02|archive-date=2019-02-07|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190207121953/https://www.news24.com/Africa/News/president-fonseca-eyes-second-term-in-cape-verde-vote-20161002|url-status=dead}}</ref>

==Candidates==
[[Jorge Carlos Fonseca]] was looking to secure his second term and was the favourite to win as the main opposition party the [[African Party for the Independence of Cape Verde]] (PAICV) failed to present a candidate after their disappointing loss in the [[2016 Cape Verdean parliamentary election|parliamentary elections]] in March and municipal elections in September. Fonseca, representing the MpD, faced two independent candidates, Joaquim Monteiro and Albertino Graça.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.africanews.com/2016/10/02/polls-open-in-cape-verde-s-presidential-election/|title=Polls open in Cape Verde's presidential election|last=AfricaNews|date=2016-10-02|website=Africanews|access-date=2016-10-02}}</ref>

==Campaign==
Campaigning was temporarily suspended on 22 September following the death of the country's ex-president [[António Mascarenhas Monteiro]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://pulse.ng/world/in-cape-verde-presidential-campaign-suspended-id5500654.html|title=In Cape Verde: Presidential campaign suspended - World - Pulse|last=AFP|first=Pulse News Agency International By|access-date=2016-10-05|archive-date=2016-10-06|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161006144319/http://pulse.ng/world/in-cape-verde-presidential-campaign-suspended-id5500654.html|url-status=dead}}</ref> Monteiro was the country's first democratically elected president and was also a member of the MpD. All public events and rallies were suspended for 4 days until 26 September.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://au.news.yahoo.com/world/a/32641064/presidential-campaign-suspended-in-cape-verde/#page1|title=Presidential campaign suspended in Cape Verde|access-date=2016-10-05|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161006051243/https://au.news.yahoo.com/world/a/32641064/presidential-campaign-suspended-in-cape-verde/#page1#page1|archive-date=2016-10-06|url-status=dead}}</ref>

